  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Shower and thunderstorm chances remain today as the front \nslowly sags south through region. Marginal to Slight Risk of severe\nstorms for most of VA and eastern WV.\n\nMesoscale complex of thunderstorms has finally organized over \nNE Indiana and the western half of Ohio. Now that it has formed,\nthe HRRR should have a decent handle on it. It has it \nprogressing southeast in the flow, reaching our SW zones in \nw-central VA and adjacent WV late morning. Should continue \nmoving across our counties there through mid-afternoon. Past \nthat in the later afternoon/evening there is more uncertainty, \nbut seems like the tstm threat will continue through that \nperiod. \n\nMonitoring\na few light showers out there this morning. Most of this \nactivity has been confined to far northeast and north- central \nMD and central VA. Also keeping an eye on a little ripple of \nenergy back across southern Michigan and northern Ohio. This \narea of showers and t-storms looks to drop south and east with \ntime later this morning and into the afternoon bringing \nadditional spotty showers and t- storms, mainly to central and \nsouthwest VA.\n\nScattered to broken mid and high level clouds to pass through with \nan increase in cloud cover heading toward daybreak as the front sags \nthrough. The front itself is currently draped to the north of the \nPA/MD line. Hi-res guidance has the front in the vicinity of the \nPotomac River later this morning and down along the I-64 corridor or \njust south of that area Saturday afternoon. As a result, expect a \ncontinuation of spotty shower chances in and around the front this \nmorning with a couple of thunderstorms this afternoon as a wave of \nenergy pushes across the front. Any severe threat appears low \nwith a Marginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) for portions of the \nsouthern Shenandaoh Valley and central VA Piedmont region. \nDamaging winds will be the main threat with storms along with \nfrequent lightning and locally heavy rainfall. \n\nHighs today will range from the upper 70s and low 80s along the \nPA/MD line to around 90 just south of Charlottesville, VA. Lows \nSaturday night will fall into the upper 50s and mid 60s as drier air \npunches into the region. Humidity will slowly lower throughout the \nday as the front pushes south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures and \nlower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic Sunday into Monday, with accompanying mid-level \ndry air that will bring down humidity and t-storm chances. Daily\nhighs drop into the low 80s, along with drier dewpoints in the \nmid to upper 50s. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid-Atlantic \non Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the Southeast. \nTemperatures likely begin to creep up again during this time, \naccompanied by increasing humidity as mid-level dry air tracks south \nof the region with more humid air coming in from the north. This \nwill correspond with strong upper-level trough forming over Quebec \naround midweek, with an accompanying cold front tracking across the \nEast Coast sometime between Wednesday and Thursday. This could \ncreate some chances for precipitation over the region, with some \nindicators for low-end instability over the area as this mid-level \nmoisture tracks southeast. Severe probabilities look low at this \ntime, but a few thunderstorms are not completely out of the question \nas this front moves through.\n\nLooking towards late next week, temperatures likely drop again in \nthe wake of this cold front Friday as it tracks to the south.",
